    "natural_completion_before_410": true,
    "output_text": "```python\ndef longest_increasing_subsequence_length(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the length of the longest strictly increasing subsequence.\n    \n    Uses binary search with a 'tails' array for O(n log n) time complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n    \n    tails = []  # tails[i] = smallest tail element for an increasing subsequence of length i+1\n    \n    for num in nums:\n        # Find the position where num should be inserted in tails\n        # Using binary search to find the leftmost element >= num\n        left, right = 0, len(tails)\n        while left < right:\n            mid = (left + right) // 2\n            if tails[mid] < num:\n                left = mid + 1\n            else:\n                right = mid\n        \n        # If num is larger than all elements in tails, append it\n        if left == len(tails):\n            tails.append(num)\n        else:\n            # Replace the first element that is >= num\n            tails[left] = num\n    \n    return len(tails)\n```\n\n**Time Complexity Explanation:**\n\nThe function runs in **O(n log n)** time, where *n* is the length of the input list. For each of the *n* elements, we perform a binary search over the `tails` array, which has at most *n* elements. Each binary search takes O(log n) time, leading to an overall O(n log n) time complexity. The space complexity is O(n) in the worst case, as the `tails` array can grow to size *n*. This is significantly better than the naive O(n\u00b2) dynamic programming approach.",
